---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Note from '../components/case/Note.astro';
import '../styles/prose.css';
---

<Base
  title="This site"
  description="Why the first version of this portfolio was retired, and how this one is built: a power switch, offline reading, a hand-built WebGL sky and very little JavaScript."
>
  <article class="page colophon">
    <header class="colophon-head">
      <p class="label">This site</p>
      <h1 class="colophon-title">The portfolio is a case study too.</h1>
      <p class="colophon-lead">
        If I say I design for failure conditions, the site should work under them. Here's what I
        changed, and why.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose colophon-body">
      <h2 id="what-died">What I retired</h2>
      <p>
        The first version was <a href="/v1/">Nostalgia OS</a>: pick Windows Vista or Mac OS X, watch it
        boot, open windows to find the work. The engineering was real. But the work sat behind a boot
        screen and three clicks, it borrowed Microsoft's and Apple's design instead of showing mine, and
        it barely worked on a phone.
      </p>
      <Note kind="decision">
        Keep v1's one good idea (the site itself should prove something) and retire the rest.
      </Note>

      <h2 id="power">The power switch</h2>
      <p>
        I design for load-shedding, so this site has a <strong>Power</strong> switch. Turn it off and
        fonts, colour and motion drop away, every diagram becomes text, and the page measures how little
        of itself you needed. Browsers that ask to save data start with it off. A service worker also
        keeps every page you've opened readable with no connection. That part isn't a simulation.
      </p>

      <h2 id="sky">The sky</h2>
      <p>
        Behind the pages is a field of stars you travel through as you scroll, and on the homepage a
        planet drawn like one of the diagrams. It's hand-written WebGL: two small shaders and no 3D
        library, so the whole sky weighs about 5 KB. It loads after the words, holds still for anyone
        who prefers reduced motion, and goes out when you cut the power. A real outage takes the
        luxuries first.
      </p>

      <h2 id="budgets">Measured</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>What</th><th>Actual</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>JavaScript, whole site</td><td>≈ 8 KB (3.5 KB gzipped), no libraries</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The sky (WebGL)</td><td>≈ 12 KB (5 KB gzipped), fetched after the page loads, only with the power on</td></tr>
          <tr><td>CSS, gzipped</td><td>≈ 12 KB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fonts</td><td>≈ 170 KB, skipped with the power off</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Images</td><td>0. Every diagram is drawn from real numbers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Security</td><td>Strict hashed content security policy, no inline styles</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="next">What I'd still improve</h2>
      <p>
        Real photographs from the field, like the auction yard or a building site mid-inspection. Until I
        have good ones, the diagrams stay honest rather than decorative.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .colophon-head {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 4vw, 5rem) var(--space-12);
  }

  .colophon-title {
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 1.4rem + 3.2vw, 4.25rem);
    font-weight: 380;
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    max-width: 16ch;
  }

  .colophon-lead {
    font-size: var(--text-lead);
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }

  .colophon-body {
    --margin-col: 15rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  @media (min-width: 72rem) {
    .colophon-body {
      margin-left: calc(12rem + var(--space-12));
    }
  }
</style>
